<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Merrimack River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Merrimack River rises in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a> and flows about 190 km south and then east through Massachusetts to the Atlantic at Newburyport. Early colonial grants used it as a boundary, and its valley was home to Pennacook and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abenaki">Abenaki</a> communities. In the 19th century its water power drove the textile mills of Lowell, Lawrence and Manchester.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/merrimack-river.jpg" alt="Merrimack River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Parkerjh · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mouth_Merrimack_River.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
